<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Candelaria River</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">Candelaria</span></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Candelaria is a river of south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> that rises in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala">Guatemala</a> and flows through the state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/campeche">Campeche</a> into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laguna-de-terminos">Laguna de Términos</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-mexico">Gulf of Mexico</a>. In the late Postclassic period its basin was the land of Acalan, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chontal-maya">Chontal Maya</a> state whose capital, Itzamkanac, lay on the river and was visited by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hernan-cortes">Hernán Cortés</a> in 1525.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/candelaria-river.jpg" alt="Candelaria River" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: ERVIN ESCOBAR · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:RIO_CANDELARIA_-_panoramio.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
